==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-exasperate.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|æspəɹeɪt|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|exasperat}}

# To [[frustrate]], [[vex]], [[provoke]], or [[annoy]]; to make [[angry]].
#* {{circa|1611}} [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''Macbeth'', act 3, sc. 6:
#*: this report
#*: Hath so '''exasperate''' the king that he
#*: Prepares for some attempt of war.
#* '''1851''', [[w:Herman Melville|Herman Melville]], ''Moby Dick'', ch. 3:
#*: The picture represents a Cape-Horner in a great hurricane; the half-foundered ship weltering there with its three dismantled masts alone visible; and an '''exasperated''' whale, purposing to spring clean over the craft, is in the enormous act of impaling himself upon the three mast-heads.
#* '''1853''', [[w:Charles Dickens|Charles Dickens]], ''Bleak House'', ch. 11:
#*: Beadle goes into various shops and parlours, examining the inhabitants; always shutting the door first, and by exclusion, delay, and general idiotcy, '''exasperating''' the public.
#* '''1987''', "[http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,963185-9,00.html Woman of the Year: Corazon Aquino]," ''Time'', 5 Jan:
#*: [S]he '''exasperates''' her security men by acting as if she were protected by some invisible shield.
#* '''2007''', "[http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/comment/leading_article/article1878668.ece Loyal Mail]," ''Times Online'' (UK), 4 June (retrieved 7 Oct 2010):
#*: News that Adam Crozier, Royal Mail chief executive, is set to receive a bumper bonus will '''exasperate''' postal workers.

